From: "A. Reader" <reader@example.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 06:04:11 +0530
Subject: [PATCH bpf-next 0/8] bpf: Add a count-min sketch map type

A program that counts events per key in a hash map needs an entry for
every key. When keys come and go without end, as thread IDs, flows or
addresses do, a hash fills and refuses new keys with -E2BIG, and an LRU
hash keeps the keys used most recently rather than the largest counts.

This series adds BPF_MAP_TYPE_COUNT_MIN, a count-min sketch (Cormode
and Muthukrishnan, "An improved data stream summary: the count-min
sketch and its applications", J. Algorithms 55(1), 2005): approximate
counts for any number of keys in 8 * width * depth bytes fixed at
creation. An estimate is never below the true count and, with
probability 1 - delta, exceeds it by at most epsilon times the total,
for width = ceil(e / epsilon) and depth = ceil(ln(1 / delta)).

The map follows the bloom filter. It is keyless; a value is a key
followed by a u64 count; bpf_map_push_elem() adds the count to the key
and bpf_map_peek_elem() returns the estimate. From user space,
BPF_MAP_UPDATE_ELEM pushes and BPF_MAP_LOOKUP_ELEM writes the estimate
into the value's count. max_entries is the width and the lower 4 bits
of map_extra the depth.

Patches 1 and 2 let a bloom filter peek a value on the stack, which the
stack liveness of v7.2 refuses ("slot poisoned by dead code
elimination"). Mainline fixed this in commit 830e4372de5c ("bpf:
Describe helper stack accesses with arg_access_info"); on bpf-next they
would be dropped and patch 3 would add its map type to that function.
Patch 3 adds the map, patch 4 documents it, patches 5 and 6 teach
libbpf and bpftool its name, patch 7 adds tests and patch 8 a
benchmark.

Numbers from an x86-64 VM with 16 CPUs (AMD Ryzen 7 8845HS, SRSO
mitigation "Safe RET"), the series on v7.2 with lock debugging off:

  bench, 100,000 keys, M ops/s per producer   1 producer   8 producers
  count-min-push (width 2719, depth 5)           47.3          19.3
  count-min-peek                                 48.2          42.1
  count-min-hash (lookup, add, insert once)      37.6          31.4
  count-min-lru                                  36.4          31.3
  bloom-update (5 hashes)                        19.6           7.4

A write counter keyed by thread ID at fentry/vfs_write, one writer,
ns per run: hash 89.5, LRU hash 94.5, count-min map 93.1, and the same
sketch written in BPF in an arena 75.9. With 100,004 threads and
700,000 writes, a 1,024-entry hash refused 296,940 inserts and an LRU
hash lost the four heaviest threads; the map, 109,064 bytes, kept an
estimate for every thread, none below the truth and none above the
bound of 700.

Push and peek compute the slot of each row inline: called as a
function, five times a push, the counter cost 108.0 ns a run and the
push benchmark 25.3 M/s.

A question for reviewers. The same sketch built in BPF on an arena
costs less per event, is read by user space without system calls and
needs no kernel change; the case for such structures in BPF code was
made in the bloom filter's review [1]. The map type gives bpf() access
to tools that load no program and a stable interface. Is that worth a
new map type, or would a sketch in the selftests' arena library be the
better home?

[1] https://lore.kernel.org/bpf/CAEf4Bza_y6497cWE5H04gDg__RkoMovkFYSqXjo-yFG7XH11ug@mail.gmail.com/

The series is based on v7.2 rather than bpf-next.

